        "You do not need laboratory terminology to keep a useful log. For every important file or object, note what it is, where it came from, original location, date/time acquired, person who handled it, and copies created. Keep original files read-only if possible and make working copies for lawyers or filing. If you send something to an authority, obtain a receipt or list it in your annexure index. This record can explain authenticity later without making unsupported claims about technical proof.",
        "Do not assume that more material is always better. A hundred irrelevant family chats may expose private third parties and hide the key message. Give your advocate a full archive but prepare a focused list of material facts and corresponding exhibits. Redaction may be necessary for copies, but preserve an unredacted original and disclose the redaction to counsel.",
